Contact surfaces, lubrication and wear as a system (@mat-wear-system: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../sources/Breviary-Technical-Ceramics.lingua#r_7998JE61RKQF7VXXYK197KRBH4", #source "../sources/Corrosion-and-Test-Interpretation-NPL.lingua#r_6SEQ1G67MES2K0JB6X7AEA4WD6", #materials-engineering "../../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/elements/Tribology-lubrication-contamination-and-wear-evidence.lingua#r_38N674EAKQ2ZE14VR5CE6S1N6W") { r_0K3ECTGJ602W6JEX0WX7CWWMNB
**Read:** [Breviary Technical Ceramics — Verband der Keramischen Industrie](../sources/Breviary-Technical-Ceramics.lingua#r_7998JE61RKQF7VXXYK197KRBH4), section 5.7; [Corrosion control and interpretation of tests — NPL](../sources/Corrosion-and-Test-Interpretation-NPL.lingua#r_6SEQ1G67MES2K0JB6X7AEA4WD6), wear and flow damage. Work the shared mechanical tribology and metallurgical wear Records.

Wear is a response of contacting bodies and their surroundings, not a single intrinsic number attached to one material. Counterface, roughness, debris, lubricant, temperature and loading history can change the dominant mechanism. Transfer films or third bodies may form during running-in, so the initial and later rates can differ.

**Work:** Describe the entire contact system and distinguish adhesion, abrasion, fatigue-related removal and tribochemical effects using observations. Close a material-loss account without assuming all removed material leaves the contact. Compare two test geometries and identify which contact pressure, speed, environment and thermal conditions can actually be matched. Relate the measured loss to clearance, accuracy or other required function.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
